---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Cite from '../components/content/Cite.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/content/Placeholder.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import ScreeningPathway from '../components/science/ScreeningPathway.astro';
import HillExplorer from '../components/science/HillExplorer.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'pipeline', title: 'The analysis pipeline' },
  { id: 'normalisation', title: 'Turning signals into comparable numbers' },
  { id: 'module-model', title: 'Modelling one module' },
  { id: 'classification', title: 'Comparing whole patterns' },
  { id: 'validation', title: 'How we would know the model is real' },
];

const refs = ['machado2005', 'nlst2011'];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="model"
  toc={toc}
  refs={refs}
  brief={[
    'The analysis turns raw reporter signals into a comparable pattern, then compares patterns rather than single values.',
    'With few samples, simple models and honest validation matter more than sophisticated ones.',
    'A screening result depends on how common the disease is in the group tested, not only on the model.',
  ]}
>
  <Section id="pipeline" title="The analysis pipeline">
    <p>
      Every step below either removes a known source of variation or makes samples comparable. Each is written down
      before data exists, so that the analysis cannot be quietly tuned until it gives a pleasing answer.
    </p>

    <ScreeningPathway
      label="Steps from a raw reporter reading to a screening suggestion"
      steps={[
        { title: 'Raw readings', detail: 'Reporter signal per module, per time point, straight from the reader.', needs: 'Plate layout and run metadata' },
        { title: 'Background correction', detail: 'Subtract unexposed-module and room-air blanks measured in the same run.', needs: 'Controls on every plate' },
        { title: 'Normalisation', detail: 'Express each module relative to its own positive control, so batches can be compared.', needs: 'A reference standard per run' },
        { title: 'Feature vector', detail: 'One number per module, ordered the same way every time: the breath signature.', needs: 'A fixed module order' },
        { title: 'Pattern comparison', detail: 'Compare with reference patterns using a model trained on separate data.', needs: 'Labelled training data' },
        { title: 'Output', detail: 'A distance from reference and, in the concept, a suggestion to refer for clinical assessment.', needs: 'Agreed thresholds' },
      ]}
    />
  </Section>

  <Section id="normalisation" title="Turning signals into comparable numbers">
    <p>
      Cells differ between batches, readers drift, and a plate measured on Tuesday is not a plate measured on Friday.
      Normalisation is what makes two measurements comparable at all.
    </p>
    <div class="equation">
      <p class="equation__math">
        r<sub>i</sub> = (s<sub>i</sub> − b<sub>i</sub>) / (p<sub>i</sub> − b<sub>i</sub>)
      </p>
      <p class="equation__key">
        For module <em>i</em>: <strong>s</strong> is the measured signal, <strong>b</strong> the blank from unexposed
        modules in the same run, and <strong>p</strong> the signal from the positive control. The result <strong>r</strong> is
        a response on a common scale.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Note kind="limitation" title="Normalisation cannot rescue a bad measurement">
      <p>
        If the blank and the sample were not measured in the same run, or the positive control saturated, no arithmetic
        afterwards will fix it. This is why controls appear in every stage of the{' '}
        <a href={href('experiments')}>experimental workflow</a>.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="module-model" title="Modelling one module">
    <p>
      Before the array can be modelled, each module needs a description of how its response changes with concentration.
      A Hill function is the usual starting point: two parameters, both with a physical meaning, both fittable from a
      dose–response curve.
    </p>
    <HillExplorer />
    <p>
      Fitted values of K and n tell us something practical: a module with a high K will not see breath-level
      concentrations at all, and a module with a very high n behaves like a switch, which is poor for measuring amounts.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="classification" title="Comparing whole patterns">
    <p>
      With a normalised vector per sample, the question becomes whether vectors from different groups separate. Two
      complementary views are useful, and neither is a classifier on its own.
    </p>
    <Table label="Planned analysis methods">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Method</th>
            <th scope="col">What it answers</th>
            <th scope="col">Why it is appropriate here</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Distance from reference</th>
            <td>How far this pattern sits from the reference pattern</td>
            <td>Simple, interpretable, and what the home page simulation shows</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Principal component analysis</th>
            <td>Whether groups separate at all, in two dimensions we can look at</td>
            <td>A visual check before any classifier, used in earlier breath studies<Cite id="machado2005" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Simple supervised classifier</th>
            <td>Whether a rule learned on one set of samples works on another</td>
            <td>With few samples, a model with few parameters is less likely to fit noise</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>
    <Note kind="limitation" title="Six modules, few samples">
      <p>
        With a small dataset it is easy to build a model that separates groups perfectly and means nothing. We plan to
        report cross-validated performance, keep a test set that is never used for tuning, and repeat the analysis with
        shuffled labels to show what chance performance looks like.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="validation" title="How we would know the model is real">
    <p>
      Screening performance is not one number. A test with high sensitivity can still produce mostly false alarms when
      the condition is rare, which is exactly what CT screening trials found: 96.4% of positive low-dose CT screens in
      the NLST were false positives.<Cite id="nlst2011" /> Any future claim about OncoNose would have to report the same
      quantities, in a population where the prevalence is stated.
    </p>
    <Table label="Measures that would have to be reported">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Measure</th>
            <th scope="col">Question it answers</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Sensitivity</th>
            <td>Of people with the condition, how many does the test flag?</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Specificity</th>
            <td>Of people without it, how many does the test correctly leave alone?</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Positive predictive value</th>
            <td>Of people flagged, how many actually have the condition? This depends on how common it is.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Reproducibility</th>
            <td>Does the same sample give the same answer on a different day, batch or device?</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

    </Table>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>

<style>
  .equation {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    margin: var(--sp-5) 0;
    padding: var(--sp-5);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--paper-sunk);
  }

  .equation__math {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-style: italic;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .equation__key {
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
